1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,486
Aarav opens an old remote control.

2
00:00:02,486 --> 00:00:04,213
He expects a nest of wires.

3
00:00:04,513 --> 00:00:09,107
Instead, he finds a flat green
board, covered in thin copper lines.

4
00:00:09,407 --> 00:00:11,223
Where did all the wires go?

5
00:00:11,223 --> 00:00:12,270
Let's find out.

6
00:00:12,770 --> 00:00:16,186
This is a printed circuit board, or PCB.

7
00:00:16,536 --> 00:00:18,666
The board itself is an insulator.

8
00:00:18,666 --> 00:00:20,645
Current cannot flow through it.

9
00:00:20,995 --> 00:00:25,581
Common boards are glass fibre with
epoxy, or a cheaper paper board with

10
00:00:25,581 --> 00:00:26,727
phenolic resin.

11
00:00:27,377 --> 00:00:30,328
On top are thin copper
paths, called tracks.

12
00:00:30,328 --> 00:00:32,138
They do the job of wires.

13
00:00:32,488 --> 00:00:35,011
At each part, a track
ends in a copper pad.

14
00:00:35,011 --> 00:00:36,809
The part's leg is soldered there.

15
00:00:37,459 --> 00:00:39,655
The green coat is the solder mask.

16
00:00:39,655 --> 00:00:42,694
It protects the copper,
and stops solder bridges.

17
00:00:43,044 --> 00:00:45,276
The white letters are the silkscreen.

18
00:00:45,276 --> 00:00:47,236
They show where each part goes.

19
00:00:47,886 --> 00:00:49,707
PC Bs come in three types.

20
00:00:50,057 --> 00:00:53,419
Single sided boards have
copper on one side only.

21
00:00:53,769 --> 00:00:56,768
Double sided boards have
copper on both sides.

22
00:00:56,768 --> 00:01:00,322
Small plated holes, called
vias, join the two sides.

23
00:01:00,972 --> 00:01:04,305
Multilayer boards hide
many copper layers inside.

24
00:01:04,305 --> 00:01:05,619
Phones use them.

25
00:01:06,269 --> 00:01:08,641
So how is a simple PCB made?

26
00:01:08,641 --> 00:01:09,760
Step by step.

27
00:01:10,110 --> 00:01:12,190
Start with a copper clad board.

28
00:01:12,190 --> 00:01:14,117
Copper covers one whole side.

29
00:01:14,467 --> 00:01:18,297
Draw the circuit layout, and print
it onto the copper with a protective

30
00:01:18,297 --> 00:01:19,197
ink.

31
00:01:19,083 --> 00:01:23,001
Dip the board in an etching
solution, such as ferric chloride.

32
00:01:23,001 --> 00:01:24,964
It eats away the bare copper.

33
00:01:25,314 --> 00:01:27,211
The copper under the ink stays.

34
00:01:27,211 --> 00:01:29,740
Clean off the ink, and the tracks appear.

35
00:01:30,390 --> 00:01:32,086
Drill holes for the parts.

36
00:01:32,086 --> 00:01:34,535
Then place the parts, and solder them.

37
00:01:35,185 --> 00:01:37,555
But a track is not a perfect wire.

38
00:01:37,555 --> 00:01:39,359
It has a small resistance.

39
00:01:39,709 --> 00:01:41,979
Remember, R equals rho L by A.

40
00:01:42,329 --> 00:01:45,356
For a track, A is the width
times the copper thickness.

41
00:01:46,006 --> 00:01:49,335
The copper is very thin,
often thirty five micrometres.

42
00:01:49,335 --> 00:01:51,174
That is thinner than a hair.

43
00:01:51,824 --> 00:01:55,927
Let's push two amperes from a five
volt supply, through a ten centimetre

44
00:01:55,927 --> 00:01:56,827
track.

45
00:01:56,836 --> 00:01:59,874
First, a thin track, a
quarter of a millimetre wide.

46
00:02:00,224 --> 00:02:03,443
The part at the end receives
only four point six one volts.

47
00:02:03,793 --> 00:02:06,343
Now make the track one millimetre wide.

48
00:02:06,693 --> 00:02:09,492
The part now receives about
four point nine volts.

49
00:02:10,142 --> 00:02:11,305
Watch the scope.

50
00:02:11,305 --> 00:02:13,536
The wider track loses less voltage.

51
00:02:14,186 --> 00:02:16,666
So tracks that carry big
currents are made wide.

52
00:02:17,316 --> 00:02:19,103
Why do we use PC Bs?

53
00:02:19,103 --> 00:02:20,588
Five big advantages.

54
00:02:20,938 --> 00:02:22,686
They are small and compact.

55
00:02:23,036 --> 00:02:26,147
There are no loose wires,
so fewer wiring mistakes.

56
00:02:26,497 --> 00:02:30,111
The same board can be copied
thousands of times, cheaply.

57
00:02:30,461 --> 00:02:33,669
Parts are held firmly, so
the circuit is reliable.

58
00:02:34,019 --> 00:02:36,251
And they are easy to test and repair.

59
00:02:36,901 --> 00:02:39,338
A track is a wire, printed flat.

60
00:02:40,138 --> 00:02:41,038
Worked example.

61
00:02:41,014 --> 00:02:44,627
A copper track is one millimetre wide,
thirty five micrometres thick, and

62
00:02:44,627 --> 00:02:45,784
ten centimetres long.

63
00:02:46,134 --> 00:02:48,394
Area A is width times thickness.

64
00:02:48,394 --> 00:02:53,138
Ten to the minus three, times thirty
five times ten to the minus six.

65
00:02:53,838 --> 00:02:57,898
That is three point five times ten
to the minus eight square metres.

66
00:02:58,598 --> 00:03:02,906
Copper's resistivity is about one point
seven times ten to the minus eight

67
00:03:02,906 --> 00:03:03,806
ohm metres.

68
00:03:04,491 --> 00:03:06,212
R equals rho L by A.

69
00:03:06,212 --> 00:03:09,621
That gives about zero
point zero four nine ohms.

70
00:03:10,322 --> 00:03:12,846
At two amperes, the drop is I times R.

71
00:03:12,846 --> 00:03:14,565
About zero point one volts.

72
00:03:15,215 --> 00:03:16,677
Now, the exam trap.

73
00:03:17,027 --> 00:03:20,027
Many students take A as
the top area of the track.

74
00:03:20,027 --> 00:03:21,240
Width times length.

75
00:03:21,590 --> 00:03:22,490
Wrong.

76
00:03:22,140 --> 00:03:25,618
Current flows along the track,
so A is the cross section.

77
00:03:26,268 --> 00:03:28,228
A equals width times thickness.

78
00:03:28,878 --> 00:03:30,499
Where is this asked?

79
00:03:30,499 --> 00:03:32,359
In PUC board papers.

80
00:03:32,709 --> 00:03:36,093
The model paper asks for
the advantages of PC Bs.

81
00:03:36,093 --> 00:03:38,594
Expect types and steps of making too.

82
00:03:39,244 --> 00:03:40,144
Try this.

83
00:03:40,039 --> 00:03:43,986
A copper track has a resistance
of zero point zero eight ohms.

84
00:03:43,986 --> 00:03:47,245
A second track is twice
as wide and half as long.

85
00:03:47,245 --> 00:03:48,835
What is its resistance?

86
00:03:54,235 --> 00:03:55,135
A.

87
00:03:54,601 --> 00:03:56,208
Zero point zero two ohms.

88
00:03:56,558 --> 00:03:58,443
R equals rho L by A.

89
00:03:58,443 --> 00:04:00,129
Halving L halves R.

90
00:04:00,479 --> 00:04:02,370
Doubling the width doubles A.

91
00:04:02,370 --> 00:04:03,779
That halves R again.

92
00:04:04,129 --> 00:04:05,922
So R becomes one quarter.

93
00:04:05,922 --> 00:04:09,778
Zero point zero eight by four
is zero point zero two ohms.

94
00:04:10,428 --> 00:04:11,974
Screenshot this card.

95
00:04:15,174 --> 00:04:18,229
Next, Class twelve begins with the JFET.

96
00:04:18,229 --> 00:04:20,495
A transistor controlled by voltage.
